---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Terms of Use – Room Planner';
const description =
  'Terms of use for Room Planner: the tool is for planning purposes, measurements depend on the dimensions you enter, and clearance suggestions are not professional building advice.';
const updated = 'September 2026';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical="/terms">
  <section class="rp-container py-12">
    <div class="rp-prose">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-ink">Terms of use</h1>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-ink-faint">Last updated: {updated}</p>

      <h2>What Room Planner is for</h2>
      <p>
        Room Planner is a lightweight planning aid. It helps you sketch a rectangular room and try
        furniture arrangements in two dimensions. It is not a CAD package, it does not produce
        construction drawings, and it is not a substitute for professional measurement or design.
      </p>

      <h2>Measurements depend on you</h2>
      <p>
        Every dimension shown by Room Planner is derived from numbers you enter. The tool performs the
        arithmetic faithfully, but it has no way to verify your measurements, and it does not know about
        skirting boards, radiators, pipe boxing, bay windows, sloping ceilings, out-of-square walls or
        anything else it was not told.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>
          Always re-measure and verify critical dimensions before buying furniture, ordering materials, or
          making any building decision.
        </strong>
      </p>

      <h2>Clearance and fit suggestions</h2>
      <p>
        The walking-space check, overlap warnings and the Will It Fit? calculator are general planning
        guidelines based on common rules of thumb. They are informational only.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>They are not an accessibility assessment.</li>
        <li>They are not a fire-safety or means-of-escape assessment.</li>
        <li>They are not a building-code or building-regulations assessment.</li>
        <li>They do not account for the third dimension, so ceiling height and item height are on you.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Where legal requirements apply — accessibility standards, egress widths, or local building
        regulations — consult the relevant code and a qualified professional.
      </p>

      <h2>Your layouts</h2>
      <p>
        Anything you create with Room Planner is yours. Layouts are stored in your own browser, so keeping
        a backup is also yours to manage: use <strong>Export layout file</strong> if a plan matters to you.
        Clearing site data, using private browsing, or switching device or browser will lose anything that
        has not been exported.
      </p>

      <h2>Availability</h2>
      <p>
        The service is provided as-is and as-available, without warranties of any kind, express or implied,
        including fitness for a particular purpose. Features may change or be removed. To the fullest
        extent permitted by law, we are not liable for any loss arising from use of this tool — including
        furniture that does not fit, materials ordered in the wrong quantity, or plans that cannot be
        recovered.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing in these terms limits any liability that cannot lawfully be limited, including liability
        for death or personal injury caused by negligence, or for fraud.
      </p>

      <h2>Acceptable use</h2>
      <p>
        Please do not attempt to disrupt the service, or use it to distribute malicious files through the
        import feature. Imported layout files are validated, but you should only import files you trust and
        recognise.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes to these terms</h2>
      <p>
        These terms may be updated as the tool changes. The date at the top of this page shows when it was
        last revised. Continuing to use Room Planner after a change means you accept the revised terms.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
